LOAD of a source file will perform a minimal compilation at load time.

COMPILE will perform the minimal compilation of the source form and
produce an interpreted function that is fully macroexpanded.

COMPILE-FILE will produce a "fast load"file containing the
macroexpanded forms (there should be no macro in the "fast load"
file). and load of a "fast load" will therefore work faster, since no
macro is to be found (but it can still check for them in case
something went wrong).


Note that minimal compilation is more than just macroexpansion. See
https://www.lispworks.com/documentation/HyperSpec/Body/03_bbb.htm for
the 3 items composing minimal compilation. 

----------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (slice 44):
- MACROEXPAND-1, MACROEXPAND, MACROEXPAND-ALL implemented (prelude). MACROEXPAND-ALL
  is the minimal-compilation code walker: result has no macros (handles quote,
  function/lambda, let/let*, setq, the, and the default subform walk).
- COMPILE-FILE now writes a real fast-load file of fully macroexpanded forms
  (no macros) -> closes compile-file-output; LOAD of it works.
- Runtime LOAD/eval already minimal-compiles lazily via macro displacement
  (slice 39): a global-macro call is expanded once and cached in place.
REMAINING: the COMPILE function (install a fully-macroexpanded interpreted
function); eager macroexpand-all at LOAD time (currently lazy via displacement).

----------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (slice 49, v1.1.6):
- COMPILE implemented in the prelude (overriding the kernel COMPILE primitive,
  which only EVAL'd the lambda).  It now MACROEXPAND-ALLs the definition before
  building the interpreted function, so the result is fully macroexpanded and
  fixed at compile time.  Supported: (compile nil lambda-expr) -> fn;
  (compile nil fn) -> fn; (compile name lambda-expr) -> install + return name;
  (compile name) -> name.  Returns (values result warnings-p failure-p).
  Verified: a macro redefined AFTER (compile nil '(lambda () (mymac 5))) does
  not change the compiled function's result (eager expansion proven).
- Decision on "eager macroexpand-all at LOAD time": LOAD keeps using lazy macro
  DISPLACEMENT (slice 39).  Forcing MACROEXPAND-ALL on every loaded top-level
  form is unsafe today because the walker is intentionally incomplete (it does
  not yet understand the local-macro binding forms MACROLET / SYMBOL-MACROLET
  — it would mishandle their headers).  Displacement reaches the same macro-free
  steady state without that risk and is the conforming choice for minimal-
  compilation-at-load.
- MACROEXPAND-ALL now also walks FLET / LABELS (keeping each local function's
  name + lambda list, expanding its body and the main body).

STILL REMAINING (kept open):
- COMPILE-FILE writing a real macro-free fast-load file: blocked by the GC-
  rooting corruption documented in issues/open/deferred-compile-file-rt-gc-
  corruption.issue (the MACROEXPAND-ALL + package-qualified PRIN1 write path).
- MACROEXPAND-ALL for the local-macro forms MACROLET / SYMBOL-MACROLET (these
  need local-macro environment tracking during the walk).

----------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (v1.4.31):
- FIXED: (compile nil '(lambda (x) ...)) signalled "FUNCTION: invalid function
  name: (FUNCTION (LAMBDA ...))".  %compile-definition wrapped the lambda in
  (function ...) *after* MACROEXPAND-ALL, but LAMBDA is itself a macro, so the
  walk already yields (function (lambda ...)); the extra wrap double-wrapped to
  (function (function (lambda ...))).  Now wraps BEFORE walking:
  (eval (macroexpand-all (list 'function definition))).  test_slice25's COMPILE
  cases (functionp / funcall / nth-value / compile 'name / compile #'fn) pass.
- STILL REMAINING (why test_slice25 stays in XFAIL_TESTS): COMPILE-FILE does not
  fire EVAL-WHEN (:COMPILE-TOPLEVEL ...) at compile time.  bocl's COMPILE-FILE is
  deliberately a no-op that returns the fast-load pathname and defers all form
  processing to LOAD-of-source (see its docstring + the GC-rooting blocker in
  deferred-compile-file-rt-gc-corruption.issue), so a :compile-toplevel form is
  never evaluated during COMPILE-FILE.  test_slice25.c:147 asserts it is.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (v1.5.0) — REMAINING ITEMS DONE, ISSUE CLOSED:
- FUNCTION-LAMBDA-EXPRESSION: real implementation (was a stub).  New kernel
  primitive %FUNCTION-LAMBDA-BODY (bocl.c) returns (available lambda-list body
  name closure-p) from a Function's stored slots, unwrapping a Closure and
  reporting closure-p when it has a non-empty environment; it checks
  BUILT-IN-FUNCTION-P *before* FUNCTIONP (a built-in is a FUNCTION subclass whose
  3rd slot is the C kernel_function pointer, not a Lisp body).  FLE reconstructs
  (LAMBDA <ll> . <body>) — the body is the MACROEXPAND-ALL'd one for a COMPILE'd
  function, so it is already macro-free — or (values NIL T name) for a built-in.
- DISASSEMBLE: writes the function's FUNCTION-LAMBDA-EXPRESSION when available
  (compiling a (LAMBDA ...) argument first), nothing for a built-in, returns NIL.
- MACROEXPAND-ALL now understands MACROLET and SYMBOL-MACROLET: it walks their
  bodies (and each macrolet expander body) instead of mishandling the definition
  headers via the default branch, and PRESERVES the special operator so the
  evaluator (eval.c) performs the lexical local-macro / symbol-macro expansion —
  which also keeps inner-binding shadowing correct without the walker tracking a
  local-macro environment itself.  This removes the "walker is intentionally
  incomplete" caveat above.
- COMPILE-FILE now fires EVAL-WHEN (:COMPILE-TOPLEVEL): it reads each top-level
  form and evaluates the bodies of (EVAL-WHEN (:COMPILE-TOPLEVEL) ...) at compile
  time (recursing through PROGN and top-level macro expansions), while still
  writing no fasl so LOAD of the source performs :LOAD-TOPLEVEL / :EXECUTE /
  ordinary forms.  A compile-time IN-PACKAGE is contained by a *PACKAGE* rebind.
  The deferred-compile-file-rt-gc-corruption blocker is resolved on master (the
  reader roots its input stream), so the earlier PRIN1-fasl GC concern no longer
  applies; this lighter approach avoids the fasl write path entirely.
- test_slice25 de-quarantined (removed from tests/Makefile XFAIL_TESTS); new
  regression tests in test_slice25.c cover FLE, DISASSEMBLE, and MACROLET /
  SYMBOL-MACROLET (via COMPILE).  Whole unit suite green.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRESS (v1.5.1) — COMPILE-FILE NOW WRITES A REAL FAST-LOAD FILE:
Follow-up to a user report: v1.5.0's COMPILE-FILE wrote NO file (it relied on
LOAD falling back to source), so (probe-file (compile-file f)) was NIL and
ASDF's compile-op / any code expecting the output file to exist broke.  Now that
the deferred-compile-file-rt-gc-corruption blocker is resolved, COMPILE-FILE
writes a genuine fast-load file:
- Each top-level form's compile-time effects fire first (%compile-file-toplevel:
  EVAL-WHEN :compile-toplevel, plus IN-PACKAGE / DEFPACKAGE / DEFMACRO /
  DEFINE-SYMBOL-MACRO so later forms read/print/expand in the right package with
  the file's own macros known), then the form is MACROEXPAND-ALL'd (macro-free)
  and PRIN1'd to the output with *PRINT-CIRCLE* on (so a gensym shared across an
  expansion round-trips as one symbol).
- The file starts with a header line ";; BOCL <version> FASL" (a Lisp comment,
  so the ordinary source loader skips it) that lets file(1) and LOAD identify a
  bocl fasl and check version compatibility, followed by an (IN-PACKAGE ...)
  pinning the load package.
- LOAD loads the fasl with the normal source loader (minimal compilation =
  macroexpansion; no macro expansion needed at load) and first checks the header
  version via %CHECK-FASL-VERSION (warns, non-fatally, on a newer MAJOR version).
- New helpers: %WRITE-FASL-FORM, %BOCL-FASL-VERSION, %CHECK-FASL-VERSION.
- Regression test test_slice25 "compile-file fasl": compile-file writes a fasl
  PROBE-FILE finds, with a version header, that LOADs correctly and whose bodies
  are macro-free (no local-macro call, no DOLIST, survives the walk).
Round-trip verified for macros (DOLIST / backquote), EVAL-WHEN, and multi-package
files (DEFPACKAGE + IN-PACKAGE), including the (load (compile-file f)) idiom.
